Template Pattern Implementation
Overview

The Template Method design pattern was applied to abstract the common behaviors of BFS and DFS.

An abstract base class named:

GraphSearchTemplate

was created to define the overall search workflow.

The common steps shared by BFS and DFS include:

Initializing the search
Tracking visited nodes
Expanding paths
Checking destination nodes
Returning the final path

The subclasses implement only the algorithm-specific behavior.

BFS Implementation

The BFS subclass overrides methods related to queue behavior.

Example:

public class BFSStrategy extends GraphSearchTemplate

BFS explores nodes level-by-level using FIFO ordering.

DFS Implementation

The DFS subclass overrides methods related to stack behavior.

Example:

public class DFSStrategy extends GraphSearchTemplate

DFS explores deeply before backtracking using LIFO ordering.

Why Template Pattern Was Used

The Template Pattern was used because BFS and DFS share many common steps but differ in how nodes are selected during traversal.

Using the template pattern helped:

Reduce duplicate code
Improve maintainability
Support future algorithms easily
Keep the design clean and reusable
